-5edd-8923-be6cf098777e","revision":1,"parentRevision":null},{"itemId":"fd8f4fe0-5d7e-5cd5-80bf-5941f8da012b","revision":1,"parentRevision":null},{"itemId":"b0cac564-3ed1-5087-86e4-2e4386ea8de4","revision":1,"parentRevision":null},{"itemId":"38bf5b23-0046-5e49-bb28-6aceb3aa35da","revision":1,"parentRevision":null},{"itemId":"407f56f9-0a82-53e1-88fe-8f0c8e38e796","revision":1,"parentRevision":null},{"itemId":"cde2d36f-4a3d-514b-8c7b-83d0b15b450a","revision":1,"parentRevision":null},{"itemId":"1672c927-5703-5d2b-a865-e28711a08029","revision":1,"parentRevision":null}]}],"overviews":[{"slug":"entrepreneurship-innovation","itemId":"ce24b613-5845-53a2-a748-815347fbc945","before":{"title":"Entrepreneurship and Innovation","body":"Introduction to entrepreneurship and innovation, from recognising opportunities and developing business ideas to funding and launching new ventures, and entrepreneurship's role in economic development.","examFocus":["Definitions of entrepreneurship and innovation","Types of entrepreneurs and their characteristics","Opportunity recognition process","Business idea evaluation","Short essay questions on economic impact","Theoretical frameworks and concepts"],"outcomes":["Define entrepreneurship and describe its scope and dimensions, and the characteristics of an entrepreneur.","Explain the traits of an entrepreneur and whether entrepreneurs are born or made.","Define innovation and its dimensions, link knowledge to innovation, and apply the principles of change management.","Identify the forms of business ownership in Nigeria, weigh their advantages and disadvantages, and outline a simple business plan.","Explain business registration in Nigeria, its process and the pitfalls to avoid, and the forms of business collaboration.","Define and distinguish micro and small enterprises, and explain their importance.","Explain the role of knowledge and skills, digital technology and e-commerce in modern entrepreneurship."],"assessment":["The exam is objective (CBT). Expect definitions, lists and their order, names, figures and Nigerian business cases.","Every topic is examinable."]},"after":{"title":"Entrepreneurship and Innovation","body":"Every topic is examinable.","outcomes":["Define entrepreneurship and describe its scope and dimensions, and the characteristics of an entrepreneur.","Explain the traits of an entrepreneur and whether entrepreneurs are born or made.","Define innovation and its dimensions, link knowledge to innovation, and apply the principles of change management.","Identify the forms of business ownership in Nigeria, weigh their advantages and disadvantages, and outline a simple business plan.","Explain business registration in Nigeria, its process and the pitfalls to avoid, and the forms of business collaboration.","Define and distinguish micro and small enterprises, and explain their importance.","Explain the role of knowledge and skills, digital technology and e-commerce in modern entrepreneurship."],"assessment":["The exam is objective (CBT). Expect definitions, lists and their order, names, figures and Nigerian business cases."]}},$owner_content_rule$);
insert into private.content_rule_chunks (rule, ordinal, chunk) values ('owner_content_rule', 26, $owner_content_rule${"slug":"principles-business-administration","itemId":"393817a6-f4f6-5563-9b22-4614aa481ad9","before":{"title":"Principles of Business Administration I","body":"Foundation course covering business functions, marketing fundamentals, organizational structure, management concepts, business environment analysis, and contemporary issues in Nigerian business administration.","topics":[{"chapter":"1-2","description":"Business Functions and Organizational Structure"},{"chapter":"3-4","description":"Marketing Fundamentals and Segmentation"},{"chapter":"5-6","description":"Management Concepts and Principles"},{"chapter":"7-8","description":"Business Environment and Strategy"},{"chapter":"9-10","description":"Contemporary Issues and Change Management in Nigeria"},{"chapter":"11-12","description":"Business Ownership, Ethics, and Corporate Governance"}],"examFocus":["Definition of business functions","Organizational structure types and benefits","Market segmentation strategies","Management theories and applications","PESTLE analysis and business environment","Contemporary challenges in Nigerian business","Business ownership types and liability","Corporate governance principles (NCCG 2018)","Leadership theories and styles","Theory explanations and definitions"],"keyTakeaways":[{"title":"The True Purpose of Business Goes Beyond Profit","description":"While classical thinkers like Adam Smith viewed businesses narrowly as profit-maximizing machines, the modern view sees businesses as social institutions responsible for value creation, employment generation, and wealth distribution for all stakeholders."},{"title":"Management is Universal and Interconnected","description":"Wherever there are people and goals, management is required. The four core functions—Planning, Organising, Leading, and Controlling—are deeply interrelated. You cannot control what you have not planned. Success is measured by Effectiveness (doing the right things) and Efficiency (using the least resources)."},{"title":"Constantly Scan the Dynamic Business Environment","description":"No business operates in a vacuum. Always apply the PESTLE framework (Political, Economic, Sociocultural, Technological, Legal, Environmental) to analyze forces shaping your industry and adapt to opportunities and threats."},{"title":"The Reality of the Nigerian Business Context","description":"As managers in Nigeria, you will face infrastructure deficits, economic volatility (inflation, FX constraints), and security threats. You must act as an \"Organizational Stabiliser\" and Change Agent while managing cultural rigidity, employee burnout, and the skills gap."},{"title":"Organizations are Open Systems Requiring Integration","description":"Systems Theory views businesses as open systems that receive inputs, transform them, and produce outputs. Cross-functional integration is critical—Marketing, Operations, HR, and Finance must work together, not in silos."},{"title":"Understand Business Ownership and Liability","description":"A Sole Proprietorship carries unlimited personal liability—your personal assets can be seized to pay business debts. Incorporating as a company (like a PLC) creates a separate legal entity and provides limited liability protection."},{"title":"Ethics, Governance, and Responsible Leadership","description":"Management is a recognized profession requiring adherence to ethical codes like NIM's. Uphold the NCCG 2018 principles: Responsibility, Accountability, Transparency, and Fairness. Strive to be a Transformational or Servant Leader who guides with honesty and empathy."}],"outcomes":["Define and explain the key operating elements and functions of business organisations.","Appraise the core management functions (planning, organising, leading and controlling) and show how they relate.","Apply foundational management knowledge to marketing, accounting, finance, human resources and operations.","Explain the impact of the internal and external environment on modern business, with particular reference to Nigeria.","Explain the forms of business ownership, their advantages, limitations and regulation.","Analyse the roles, skills and responsibilities of managers as change agents and leaders.","Evaluate corporate governance principles and ethical leadership practices.","Learn from the careers, leadership styles and decisions of successful Nigerian and global managers."],"assessment":["Expect a test and an examination, mostly objective questions, plus possible short quizzes during the semester.","Expect definitions, lists, theories and who proposed them, Nigerian examples and facts about the managers studied.","Every topic is examinable."]},"after":{"title":"Principles of Business Administration I","body":"Every topic is examinable.","outcomes":["Define and explain the key operating elements and functions of business organisations.","Appraise the core management functions (planning, organising, leading and controlling) and show how they relate.","Apply foundational management knowledge to marketing, accounting, finance, human resources and operations.","Explain the impact of the internal and external environment on modern business, with particular reference to Nigeria.","Explain the forms of business ownership, their advantages, limitations and regulation.","Analyse the roles, skills and responsibilities of managers as change agents and leaders.","Evaluate corporate governance principles and ethical leadership practices.","Learn from the careers, leadership styles and decisions of successful Nigerian and global managers."],"assessment":["Expect a test and an examination, mostly objective questions, plus possible short quizzes during the semester.","Expect definitions, lists, theories and who proposed them, Nigerian examples and facts about the managers studied."]}},{"slug":"leadership-governance","itemId":"22b198d9-ebcc-5801-8874-d9dd325124e5","before":{"title":"Leadership and Governance","body":"Leadership theories and styles, organisational ethics, governance frameworks, and stakeholder management across the public and private sectors.","examFocus":["Leadership theory definitions and applications","Leadership styles and situational leadership","Ethical leadership principles","Governance structures and responsibilities","Stakeholder analysis and management","Essays on organizational leadership"],"outcomes":["Discuss the meaning of leadership and governance.","Identify the basic concepts and elements of governance.","Explain governance in the public sector and its challenges.","Identify gaps in public governance and initiatives to improve them.","Identify traditional and contemporary leadership theories and styles.","Apply moral philosophies to leadership and governance problems.","Explain how citizens and civil society can be engaged in governance reform.","Take an active part in public sector reform in Nigeria."],"assessment":["Expect objective questions, with possible short theory questions. Expect definitions, lists,$owner_content_rule$);
insert into private.content_rule_chunks (rule, ordinal, chunk) values ('owner_content_rule', 27, $owner_content_rule$ theories and who proposed them, and Nigerian applications.","Every topic is examinable."]},"after":{"title":"Leadership and Governance","body":"Every topic is examinable.","outcomes":["Discuss the meaning of leadership and governance.","Identify the basic concepts and elements of governance.","Explain governance in the public sector and its challenges.","Identify gaps in public governance and initiatives to improve them.","Identify traditional and contemporary leadership theories and styles.","Apply moral philosophies to leadership and governance problems.","Explain how citizens and civil society can be engaged in governance reform.","Take an active part in public sector reform in Nigeria."],"assessment":["Expect objective questions, with possible short theory questions. Expect definitions, lists, theories and who proposed them, and Nigerian applications."]}},{"slug":"financial-accounting-1","itemId":"fc9a0adc-5f6f-5c98-a35f-7473c94a400a","before":{"title":"Financial Accounting I","body":"Fundamentals of financial accounting under IFRS: the IASB conceptual framework and the key standards governing revenue, financial-statement presentation, inventories, accounting policies, property, plant and equipment, government grants, and borrowing costs.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"IASB Framework for the Preparation and Presentation of Financial Statements"},{"chapter":"2","description":"IFRS 15 - Revenue from Contracts with Customers"},{"chapter":"3","description":"IAS 1 - Presentation of Financial Statements"},{"chapter":"4","description":"IAS 2 - Accounting for Inventory"},{"chapter":"5","description":"IAS 8 - Accounting Policies, Changes in Estimates and Errors"},{"chapter":"6","description":"IAS 16 - Property, Plant, and Equipment (PPE)"},{"chapter":"7","description":"IAS 20 - Government Grants"},{"chapter":"8","description":"IAS 23 - Borrowing Costs"}],"examFocus":["IASB Framework qualitative characteristics","IFRS 15 five-step revenue recognition model","IAS 1 complete set of financial statements","IAS 2 inventory valuation (lower of cost and NRV)","IAS 8 retrospective vs prospective application","IAS 16 depreciation calculations (straight-line & reducing balance)","IAS 20 government grants recognition rules","IAS 23 borrowing costs capitalization"],"outcomes":["Explain the purpose of the Conceptual Framework, the qualitative characteristics, the elements, recognition and measurement.","Apply the five-step model of IFRS 15 to recognise revenue.","Prepare and present financial statements in line with IAS 1.","Measure inventories at the lower of cost and net realisable value under IAS 2.","Account for changes in accounting policies and estimates, and correct prior period errors, under IAS 8.","Recognise, measure, depreciate, revalue and derecognise property, plant and equipment under IAS 16.","Account for government grants under IAS 20.","Capitalise borrowing costs on qualifying assets under IAS 23."],"assessment":["Expect objective questions and calculations on each standard.","Calculations come up in every topic. Work through every worked example until you can do them quickly.","Every topic is examinable."]},"after":{"title":"Financial Accounting I","body":"Every topic is examinable.","outcomes":["Explain the purpose of the Conceptual Framework, the qualitative characteristics, the elements, recognition and measurement.","Apply the five-step model of IFRS 15 to recognise revenue.","Prepare and present financial statements in line with IAS 1.","Measure inventories at the lower of cost and net realisable value under IAS 2.","Account for changes in accounting policies and estimates, and correct prior period errors, under IAS 8.","Recognise, measure, depreciate, revalue and derecognise property, plant and equipment under IAS 16.","Account for government grants under IAS 20.","Capitalise borrowing costs on qualifying assets under IAS 23."],"assessment":["Expect objective questions and calculations on each standard.","Calculations come up in every topic. Work through every worked example until you can do them quickly."]}},{"slug":"use-of-english","itemId":"684b4900-fe11-57fe-9c14-d7270a801804","before":{"title":"Communication in English","body":"Communication skills course covering listening, the sounds of English, stress and intonation, reading and comprehension, writing, grammar, vocabulary, and using the library and information sources.","outcomes":["Listen effectively in lectures and take notes that capture the essence of what is said.","Describe the organs of speech and identify the consonants, vowels and diphthongs of English with their symbols.","Divide words into syllables, place stress correctly and use the intonation tunes of English.","Read efficiently, using skimming, scanning and study reading, and avoid the common reading faults.","Answer comprehension questions and write a summary or synopsis.","Use study-reading methods such as SQ3R and explain how memory works.","Write a well-formed paragraph with a clear topic sentence, unity and coherence.","Write the main types of essay and punctuate correctly.","Build vocabulary and explain how English words are formed.","Identify word classes, phrases, clauses and sentence types, and avoid common sentence errors.","Use the library, its resources and its retrieval tools, and search electronic sources well.","Avoid plagiarism and cite sources in APA, Harvard and MLA style."],"assessment":["The exam is objective (CBT). Questions test definitions, lists and counts, examples, symbols and the exact wording used in this course.","Every topic is examinable."]},"after":{"title":"Communication in English","body":"Every topic is examinable.","outcomes":["Listen effectively in lectures and take notes that capture the essence of what is said.","Describe the organs of speech and identify the consonants, vowels and diphthongs of English with their symbols.","Divide words into syllables, place stress correctly and use the intonation tunes of English.","Read efficiently, using skimming, scanning and study reading, and avoid the common reading faults.","Answer comprehension questions and write a summary or synopsis.","Use study-reading methods such as SQ3R and explain how memory works.","Write a well-formed paragraph with a clear topic sentence, unity and coherence.","Write the main types of essay and punctuate correctly.","Build vocabulary and explain how English words are formed.","Identify word classes, phrases, clauses and sentence types, and avoid common sentence errors.","Use the library, its resources and its retrieval tools, and search electronic sources well.","Avoid plagiarism and cite sources in APA, Harvard and MLA style."],"assessment":["The exam is objective (CBT). Questions test definitions, lists and counts, examples, symbols and the exact wording used in this course."]}},{"slug":"nigerian-peoples-and-culture","itemId":"bba5d149-4d82-5584-a1e9-c89fc82e7c84","before":{"title":"Nigerian Peoples and Culture","body"$owner_content_rule$);
insert into private.content_rule_chunks (rule, ordinal, chunk) values ('owner_content_rule', 28, $owner_content_rule$:"A General Studies course surveying Nigerian peoples and culture. It covers the concept, characteristics and components of culture; an ethno-historical survey of Nigeria's major groups and geopolitical zones; the roles of Christianity and Islam in political culture; gender and culture; the Nigerian Civil War; and the political economy from agriculture to oil.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"Culture and Society"},{"chapter":"2","description":"Nigeria: Ethno-Historical Survey"},{"chapter":"3","description":"Historical Foundation of Nigerian Culture"},{"chapter":"4","description":"Christianity and Political Culture"},{"chapter":"5","description":"Islam and Political Culture"},{"chapter":"6","description":"Nigerian Cultural Heritage: Marriage and Naming"},{"chapter":"7","description":"Gender and Culture"},{"chapter":"8","description":"Political Economy and Intergroup Relations"}],"examFocus":["Concept, characteristics and components of culture","Geopolitical zones and major ethnic groups of Nigeria","Introduction of Christianity and the role of Badagry","Spread of Islam in Kanem-Borno and Hausaland","Pre-colonial women and the Aba Women's Riot of 1929","The Nigerian Civil War: causes, key figures and outcomes","Political economy: agriculture, oil at Oloibiri, and national development","The 1914 amalgamation and intergroup relations"]},"after":{"title":"Nigerian Peoples and Culture","body":"A General Studies course surveying Nigerian peoples and culture. It covers the concept, characteristics and components of culture; an ethno-historical survey of Nigeria's major groups and geopolitical zones; the roles of Christianity and Islam in political culture; gender and culture; the Nigerian Civil War; and the political economy from agriculture to oil.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"Culture and Society"},{"chapter":"2","description":"Nigeria: Ethno-Historical Survey"},{"chapter":"3","description":"Historical Foundation of Nigerian Culture"},{"chapter":"4","description":"Christianity and Political Culture"},{"chapter":"5","description":"Islam and Political Culture"},{"chapter":"6","description":"Nigerian Cultural Heritage: Marriage and Naming"},{"chapter":"7","description":"Gender and Culture"},{"chapter":"8","description":"Political Economy and Intergroup Relations"}]}},{"slug":"international-organisational-behaviour","itemId":"31430adf-b11f-5f51-8f58-cb27f8156d28","before":{"title":"International Organisational Behaviour","body":"A foundational course examining how culture, defined as the collective programming of the mind, shapes organisational behaviour across borders. The course moves from core cultural frameworks (Hofstede, GLOBE, Trompenaars) through perception, motivation, personality, communication, team management, leadership, negotiation, HR practice, organisational structure, ethics, and future trends including global mobility and AI. Assumes introductory Organisational Behaviour and Management.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"The Imperative for International OB — Culture, the Onion Model, Values vs Practices"},{"chapter":"2","description":"Cultural Frameworks I — Hofstede's Dimensions, Schwartz's Values, the Lewis Model"},{"chapter":"3","description":"Cultural Frameworks II — The GLOBE Study, Trompenaars' Dilemmas, the Meyer Culture Map"},{"chapter":"4","description":"Perception, Attribution, and Attitudes Across Cultures"},{"chapter":"5","description":"Motivation in Cross-Cultural Context — Maslow, Herzberg, Equity Theory"},{"chapter":"6","description":"Personality and Cultural Intelligence (CQ) — The Big Five across cultures"},{"chapter":"7","description":"Communication Styles — High-Context vs Low-Context"},{"chapter":"8","description":"Managing Multicultural and Virtual Teams — Trust and Conflict"},{"chapter":"9","description":"Cross-Cultural Leadership — Universal vs Culturally-Contingent Perspectives"},{"chapter":"10","description":"Cross-Cultural Negotiation and Decision-Making"},{"chapter":"11","description":"International Human Resource Management — Expatriate Selection and Culture Shock"},{"chapter":"12","description":"Organisational Structure, Culture and Change — Pyramids, Machines, Markets, Families"},{"chapter":"13","description":"Ethics, CSR, and Sustainability in a Global Economy"},{"chapter":"14","description":"Course Integration and Future Trends — Global Mobility, Digital Nomads, and AI"}],"examFocus":["The distinction between Values (invisible, stable) and Practices (visible, changeable)","Why transferring Western management models to other countries often fails","Definitions and real-world examples of High vs Low Power Distance","Why Individualism correlates with national wealth and Collectivism with poverty","Comparing Hofstede's dimensions with GLOBE's (e.g., institutional vs in-group collectivism)","How locus of control (internal vs external) changes with Uncertainty Avoidance","Why US motivation theories (Maslow, Herzberg) fail outside Western/individualist contexts","How Individualism vs Collectivism alters the peak of Maslow's pyramid","The difference between an individual's personality and a nation's collective culture","Edward T. Hall's High vs Low Context theory and its mapping to Individualism/Collectivism","How Masculinity/Femininity dictates conflict resolution style in teams","Why participative management (e.g., MBO) fails in High Power Distance countries","The role of the Go-between or mediator in Collectivist business negotiations","The four phases of the Acculturation Curve","Why standard performance appraisals fail in collectivist societies (loss of face)","Mapping the Pyramid, Machine, Market, and Family structures to PDI and UAI dimensions","The correlation between cultural Femininity, development aid, and environmental protection","The illusion of the \"Global Village\" — technology does not erase cultural differences"]},"after":{"title":"International Organisational Behaviour","body":"A foundational course examining how culture, defined as the collective programming of the mind, shapes organisational behaviour across borders. The course moves from core cultural frameworks (Hofstede, GLOBE, Trompenaars) through perception, motivation, personality, communication, team management, leadership, negotiation, HR practice, organisational structure, ethics, and future trends including global mobility and AI. Assumes introductory Organisational Behaviour and Management.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"The Imperative for International OB — Culture, the Onion Model, Values vs Practices"},{"chapter":"2","description":"Cultural Frameworks I — Hofstede's Dimensions, Schwartz's Values, the Lewis Model"},{"chapter":"3","description":"Cultural Frameworks II — The GLOBE Study, Trompenaars' Dilemmas, the Meyer Culture Map"},{"chapter":"4","description":"Perception, Attribution, and Attitudes Across Cultures"},{"chapter":"5","description":"Motivation in Cross-Cultural Context — M$owner_content_rule$);
insert into private.content_rule_chunks (rule, ordinal, chunk) values ('owner_content_rule', 29, $owner_content_rule$aslow, Herzberg, Equity Theory"},{"chapter":"6","description":"Personality and Cultural Intelligence (CQ) — The Big Five across cultures"},{"chapter":"7","description":"Communication Styles — High-Context vs Low-Context"},{"chapter":"8","description":"Managing Multicultural and Virtual Teams — Trust and Conflict"},{"chapter":"9","description":"Cross-Cultural Leadership — Universal vs Culturally-Contingent Perspectives"},{"chapter":"10","description":"Cross-Cultural Negotiation and Decision-Making"},{"chapter":"11","description":"International Human Resource Management — Expatriate Selection and Culture Shock"},{"chapter":"12","description":"Organisational Structure, Culture and Change — Pyramids, Machines, Markets, Families"},{"chapter":"13","description":"Ethics, CSR, and Sustainability in a Global Economy"},{"chapter":"14","description":"Course Integration and Future Trends — Global Mobility, Digital Nomads, and AI"}]}},{"slug":"principles-business-administration-2","itemId":"577bde2b-9ef5-521c-b103-b65737b7b097","before":{"title":"Principles of Business Administration II","body":"Covers how businesses manage people, change, decisions, communication and ethics, and how the Nigerian financial system and securities market fund them.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"},{"chapter":"2","description":"Human Resource Planning, Recruitment, Selection, and Employee Development"},{"chapter":"3","description":"Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management"},{"chapter":"4","description":"Organisational Change and Change Management"},{"chapter":"5","description":"Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"},{"chapter":"6","description":"Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"},{"chapter":"7","description":"Organisational Culture, Power, Politics, and Workplace Dynamics"},{"chapter":"8","description":"Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"},{"chapter":"9","description":"Business Ethics, Corporate Responsibility, and Ethical Decision-Making"},{"chapter":"10","description":"Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}],"examFocus":["Difference between Personnel Management and HRM","Objectives and functions of HRM","Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs (five levels, ordering, examples)","Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory — Hygiene (context) vs Motivator (content) factors","Lewin's Change Model and Kotter's Eight-Step Model","Drivers of and resistance to organisational change","Fields' (2000) three levels of organisational change (implemental, strategic, org-wide)","Types of managerial decisions: strategic, tactical, operational","Rationality and bounded rationality in decision-making","Nature and scope of organisational behaviour (individual, group, organisational levels)","Stages of group development (forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning)","Formal vs informal groups; command group vs task group","Barriers to effective communication; formal vs informal channels","Structure of the Nigerian financial system (CBN, NDIC, SEC, NAICOM, PenCom)","Financial intermediation and its key functions","Expansionary vs contractionary monetary policy and business impact","Shares, bonds, debentures, and mutual funds — definitions and risk/reward","Sources of business finance: equity finance vs debt finance vs venture capital","NGX Group structure and capital market intermediaries (SEC, CSCS, NGX RegCo)","Ethical theories, CSR, and workplace ethical issues"]},"after":{"title":"Principles of Business Administration II","body":"Covers how businesses manage people, change, decisions, communication and ethics, and how the Nigerian financial system and securities market fund them.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"},{"chapter":"2","description":"Human Resource Planning, Recruitment, Selection, and Employee Development"},{"chapter":"3","description":"Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management"},{"chapter":"4","description":"Organisational Change and Change Management"},{"chapter":"5","description":"Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"},{"chapter":"6","description":"Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"},{"chapter":"7","description":"Organisational Culture, Power, Politics, and Workplace Dynamics"},{"chapter":"8","description":"Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"},{"chapter":"9","description":"Business Ethics, Corporate Responsibility, and Ethical Decision-Making"},{"chapter":"10","description":"Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}]}},{"slug":"quantitative-analysis-management","itemId":"88bb9b97-531e-513d-971f-6277d4e6223e","before":{"title":"Quantitative Analysis in Management","body":"A foundational course examining how mathematical and statistical techniques support evidence-based managerial decision-making. The course moves from core quantitative concepts (break-even analysis, revenue/cost/profit formulas) through data collection, descriptive and inferential statistics, correlation and regression, linear programming, and decision theory under risk and uncertainty. Assumes Business Statistics.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"Week 1: Introduction to Quantitative Analysis in Management — Definition, Features, Importance, Areas of Application, Analytical Tools"},{"chapter":"2","description":"Week 2: Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management — Core Concepts, Revenue/Cost/Profit Formulas, Break-Even Analysis"},{"chapter":"3","description":"Week 3: Data Collection, Organization, and Presentation — Primary vs Secondary Data, Quantitative vs Qualitative, Charts and Graphs"},{"chapter":"4","description":"Week 4: Statistical Analysis for Management — Foundations of Statistics, Descriptive Statistics, the Normal Distribution, Hypothesis Testing, Confidence Intervals, ANOVA"},{"chapter":"5","description":"Week 5: Operational Modelling — Linear Programming, the Graphical Solution Method, Applications"},{"chapter":"6","description":"Week 6: Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making — Decision Environments, Criteria Under Uncertainty, EMV, Decision Trees"},{"chapter":"7","description":"Week 7: Analysis and Interpretation of Results — Data Analysis vs Interpretation, Correlation Analysis, Regression Analysis, Common Pitfalls"}],"examFocus":["Know the definition of QAM, its six features, and its five areas of application; distinguish QAM from purely intuitive decision-making","Calculate TR, TC, Profit, BEQ, BER, and Target Profit Output; solve simultaneous equations for product-mix problems; apply FV = P(1 + r)ⁿ","Distinguish primary vs secondary data and quantitative vs qualitative characteristics; construct a frequency table and identify the correct chart type for a scenario","Know the three classical definitions of statistics (Bowley, King, Boddington) and t$owner_content_rule$);
insert into private.content_rule_chunks (rule, ordinal, chunk) values ('owner_content_rule', 30, $owner_content_rule$he difference between descriptive and inferential statistics","Know when to use the Z-test vs t-test vs chi-square vs ANOVA; state H0 and H1 for any scenario; understand Type I and Type II errors; know the 68-95-99.7 rule","Formulate a linear programming problem and solve it graphically — identify the feasible region, evaluate all corner points, and state the optimal solution","Construct a payoff table, apply all five uncertainty criteria, calculate EMV, and solve a decision tree using the rollback method","Distinguish data analysis from data interpretation; interpret a regression equation (intercept, slope, R²) in plain business language","Know correlation vs causation and the other three common data-interpretation pitfalls (confirmation bias, small sample size, data quality issues)","Know the types of correlation (positive/negative, simple/multiple, partial/total, linear/non-linear) and when to use Pearson vs Spearman"],"formulaSheet":[{"name":"Break-Even Quantity (BEQ)","formula":"BEQ = FC / (P − v)","explanation":"The output level at which total revenue equals total cost — fixed cost divided by the contribution margin per unit.","example":"Selling Price = ₦5,000, Variable Cost = ₦3,000, Fixed Cost = ₦1,000,000; Contribution Margin = 5,000 − 3,000 = ₦2,000 per unit; BEQ = 1,000,000 / 2,000 = 500 units."},{"name":"Break-Even Revenue (BER)","formula":"BER = BEQ × P","explanation":"The sales revenue generated at the break-even point — break-even quantity multiplied by selling price."},{"name":"Total Revenue (TR)","formula":"TR = P × Q","explanation":"Total money earned from sales before deducting any cost — unit selling price times quantity sold.","example":"A company sells 2,500 units at ₦8,000 each: TR = 2,500 × ₦8,000 = ₦20,000,000."},{"name":"Total Cost (TC)","formula":"TC = FC + (v × Q)","explanation":"All costs combined — fixed cost plus the variable cost per unit times the number of units produced."},{"name":"Profit (π)","formula":"Profit = TR − TC = Q(P − v) − FC","explanation":"What remains after all costs: positive is a profit, negative is a loss — equivalently, contribution per unit times quantity, less fixed cost."},{"name":"Contribution Margin (CM)","formula":"CM = P − v","explanation":"The amount each unit sold contributes toward covering fixed costs (and then profit) — price minus variable cost per unit.","example":"Contribution Margin = 5,000 − 3,000 = ₦2,000 per unit."},{"name":"Average Cost (AC)","formula":"AC = TC / Q","explanation":"The cost per unit at a given output level — total cost divided by quantity."},{"name":"Marginal Cost (MC)","formula":"MC = change in TC / change in Q","explanation":"The cost of producing one additional unit — the change in total cost divided by the change in quantity."},{"name":"Target Profit Output","formula":"Target Profit Output = (FC + Target Profit) / (P − v)","explanation":"The number of units needed to reach a desired profit — fixed cost plus target profit, divided by contribution margin per unit.","example":"Target profit of ₦25,000 (FC = ₦45,000,000, P = ₦9,000, v = ₦6,000): Q = (45,000,000 + 25,000) / 3,000 = 15,009 units."},{"name":"Future Value (Compound Interest)","formula":"FV = P(1 + r)ⁿ","explanation":"The value of a present sum after n periods of compound growth at rate r per period.","example":"An investment of ₦1,000,000 growing at 10% annually for 3 years: FV = 1,000,000 × (1.10)³ = 1,000,000 × 1.331 = ₦1,331,000."},{"name":"Mean (x̄)","formula":"x̄ = Σ(x) / n","explanation":"The arithmetic average — the sum of all values divided by the number of values."},{"name":"Variance (s²)","formula":"s² = Σ(x − x̄)² / n","explanation":"The average of the squared deviations from the mean — a measure of how spread out the data is."},{"name":"Standard Deviation (s)","formula":"s = √[ Σ(x − x̄)² / n ]","explanation":"The square root of the variance — spread of the data in the same units as the data."},{"name":"Range","formula":"Range = Max − Min","explanation":"The simplest measure of spread — the largest value minus the smallest."},{"name":"Interquartile Range (IQR)","formula":"IQR = Q3 − Q1","explanation":"The spread of the middle 50% of the data — the third quartile minus the first quartile."},{"name":"Z-Score","formula":"Z = (X − μ) / σ","explanation":"How many standard deviations a value X lies from the mean — used with the normal distribution."},{"name":"Confidence Interval (CI)","formula":"CI = x̄ ± z × (σ / √(n))","explanation":"A range likely to contain the true population mean, at a chosen confidence level (z)."},{"name":"ANOVA F-Statistic","formula":"F = MSB / MSW","explanation":"Tests whether the means of three or more groups differ — the ratio of Mean Square Between to Mean Square Within; reject H0 if F-calculated > F-critical."},{"name":"Linear Programming (Objective & Corner-Point Method)","formula":"Maximise / Minimise Z = cx + dy  (evaluate Z at each corner point of the feasible region)","explanation":"Optimise an objective subject to linear constraints; the optimum occurs at a corner (vertex) of the feasible region, found by evaluating Z at every corner.","example":"Maximise Z = 5x + 4y subject to 6x + 4y ≤ 24, x + 2y ≤ 6, x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0. Corner points: (0,0) → 0, (4,0) → 20, (3,1.5) → 21, (0,3) → 12. Optimal: x = 3, y = 1.5, Z = 21."},{"name":"Expected Monetary Value (EMV)","formula":"EMV = Σ [ P(state) × Payoff(state) ]","explanation":"The probability-weighted average payoff of a decision alternative; choose the alternative with the highest EMV.","example":"Large Plant: 0.6(200,000) + 0.4(−180,000) = ₦48,000; Small Plant: 0.6(100,000) + 0.4(−20,000) = ₦52,000 → build the Small Plant (highest EMV of ₦52,000)."},{"name":"Pearson's Correlation Coefficient (r)","formula":"r = Σ[(x − x̄)(y − ȳ)] / √{ Σ(x − x̄)² × Σ(y − ȳ)² }","explanation":"Measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two variables, from −1 to +1; correlation does not imply causation.","example":"n=5, Σx=15, Σy=28, Σx²=55, Σy²=190, Σxy=102: r = [5(102) − (15)(28)] / √{[5(55) − 15²][5(190) − 28²]} = [510 − 420] / √{[275 − 225][950 − 784]} = 90 / √(50 × 166) = 90 / √8300 = 90 / 91.10 ≈ 0.988 (strong positive relationship)."}]},"after":{"title":"Quantitative Analysis in Management","body":"A foundational course examining how mathematical and statistical techniques support evidence-based managerial decision-making. The course moves from core quantitative concepts (break-even analysis, revenue/cost/profit formulas) through data collection, descriptive and inferential statistics, correlation and regression, linear programming, and decision theory under risk and uncertainty. Assumes Business Statistics.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"Week 1: Introduction to Quantitative Analysis in Management — Definition$owner_content_rule$);
insert into private.content_rule_chunks (rule, ordinal, chunk) values ('owner_content_rule', 31, $owner_content_rule$, Features, Importance, Areas of Application, Analytical Tools"},{"chapter":"2","description":"Week 2: Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management — Core Concepts, Revenue/Cost/Profit Formulas, Break-Even Analysis"},{"chapter":"3","description":"Week 3: Data Collection, Organization, and Presentation — Primary vs Secondary Data, Quantitative vs Qualitative, Charts and Graphs"},{"chapter":"4","description":"Week 4: Statistical Analysis for Management — Foundations of Statistics, Descriptive Statistics, the Normal Distribution, Hypothesis Testing, Confidence Intervals, ANOVA"},{"chapter":"5","description":"Week 5: Operational Modelling — Linear Programming, the Graphical Solution Method, Applications"},{"chapter":"6","description":"Week 6: Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making — Decision Environments, Criteria Under Uncertainty, EMV, Decision Trees"},{"chapter":"7","description":"Week 7: Analysis and Interpretation of Results — Data Analysis vs Interpretation, Correlation Analysis, Regression Analysis, Common Pitfalls"}],"formulaSheet":[{"name":"Break-Even Quantity (BEQ)","formula":"BEQ = FC / (P − v)","explanation":"The output level at which total revenue equals total cost — fixed cost divided by the contribution margin per unit.","example":"Selling Price = ₦5,000, Variable Cost = ₦3,000, Fixed Cost = ₦1,000,000; Contribution Margin = 5,000 − 3,000 = ₦2,000 per unit; BEQ = 1,000,000 / 2,000 = 500 units."},{"name":"Break-Even Revenue (BER)","formula":"BER = BEQ × P","explanation":"The sales revenue generated at the break-even point — break-even quantity multiplied by selling price."},{"name":"Total Revenue (TR)","formula":"TR = P × Q","explanation":"Total money earned from sales before deducting any cost — unit selling price times quantity sold.","example":"A company sells 2,500 units at ₦8,000 each: TR = 2,500 × ₦8,000 = ₦20,000,000."},{"name":"Total Cost (TC)","formula":"TC = FC + (v × Q)","explanation":"All costs combined — fixed cost plus the variable cost per unit times the number of units produced."},{"name":"Profit (π)","formula":"Profit = TR − TC = Q(P − v) − FC","explanation":"What remains after all costs: positive is a profit, negative is a loss — equivalently, contribution per unit times quantity, less fixed cost."},{"name":"Contribution Margin (CM)","formula":"CM = P − v","explanation":"The amount each unit sold contributes toward covering fixed costs (and then profit) — price minus variable cost per unit.","example":"Contribution Margin = 5,000 − 3,000 = ₦2,000 per unit."},{"name":"Average Cost (AC)","formula":"AC = TC / Q","explanation":"The cost per unit at a given output level — total cost divided by quantity."},{"name":"Marginal Cost (MC)","formula":"MC = change in TC / change in Q","explanation":"The cost of producing one additional unit — the change in total cost divided by the change in quantity."},{"name":"Target Profit Output","formula":"Target Profit Output = (FC + Target Profit) / (P − v)","explanation":"The number of units needed to reach a desired profit — fixed cost plus target profit, divided by contribution margin per unit.","example":"Target profit of ₦25,000 (FC = ₦45,000,000, P = ₦9,000, v = ₦6,000): Q = (45,000,000 + 25,000) / 3,000 = 15,009 units."},{"name":"Future Value (Compound Interest)","formula":"FV = P(1 + r)ⁿ","explanation":"The value of a present sum after n periods of compound growth at rate r per period.","example":"An investment of ₦1,000,000 growing at 10% annually for 3 years: FV = 1,000,000 × (1.10)³ = 1,000,000 × 1.331 = ₦1,331,000."},{"name":"Mean (x̄)","formula":"x̄ = Σ(x) / n","explanation":"The arithmetic average — the sum of all values divided by the number of values."},{"name":"Variance (s²)","formula":"s² = Σ(x − x̄)² / n","explanation":"The average of the squared deviations from the mean — a measure of how spread out the data is."},{"name":"Standard Deviation (s)","formula":"s = √[ Σ(x − x̄)² / n ]","explanation":"The square root of the variance — spread of the data in the same units as the data."},{"name":"Range","formula":"Range = Max − Min","explanation":"The simplest measure of spread — the largest value minus the smallest."},{"name":"Interquartile Range (IQR)","formula":"IQR = Q3 − Q1","explanation":"The spread of the middle 50% of the data — the third quartile minus the first quartile."},{"name":"Z-Score","formula":"Z = (X − μ) / σ","explanation":"How many standard deviations a value X lies from the mean — used with the normal distribution."},{"name":"Confidence Interval (CI)","formula":"CI = x̄ ± z × (σ / √(n))","explanation":"A range likely to contain the true population mean, at a chosen confidence level (z)."},{"name":"ANOVA F-Statistic","formula":"F = MSB / MSW","explanation":"Tests whether the means of three or more groups differ — the ratio of Mean Square Between to Mean Square Within; reject H0 if F-calculated > F-critical."},{"name":"Linear Programming (Objective & Corner-Point Method)","formula":"Maximise / Minimise Z = cx + dy  (evaluate Z at each corner point of the feasible region)","explanation":"Optimise an objective subject to linear constraints; the optimum occurs at a corner (vertex) of the feasible region, found by evaluating Z at every corner.","example":"Maximise Z = 5x + 4y subject to 6x + 4y ≤ 24, x + 2y ≤ 6, x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0. Corner points: (0,0) → 0, (4,0) → 20, (3,1.5) → 21, (0,3) → 12. Optimal: x = 3, y = 1.5, Z = 21."},{"name":"Expected Monetary Value (EMV)","formula":"EMV = Σ [ P(state) × Payoff(state) ]","explanation":"The probability-weighted average payoff of a decision alternative; choose the alternative with the highest EMV.","example":"Large Plant: 0.6(200,000) + 0.4(−180,000) = ₦48,000; Small Plant: 0.6(100,000) + 0.4(−20,000) = ₦52,000 → build the Small Plant (highest EMV of ₦52,000)."},{"name":"Pearson's Correlation Coefficient (r)","formula":"r = Σ[(x − x̄)(y − ȳ)] / √{ Σ(x − x̄)² × Σ(y − ȳ)² }","explanation":"Measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two variables, from −1 to +1; correlation does not imply causation.","example":"n=5, Σx=15, Σy=28, Σx²=55, Σy²=190, Σxy=102: r = [5(102) − (15)(28)] / √{[5(55) − 15²][5(190) − 28²]} = [510 − 420] / √{[275 − 225][950 − 784]} = 90 / √(50 × 166) = 90 / √8300 = 90 / 91.10 ≈ 0.988 (strong positive relationship)."}]}},{"slug":"philosophy-logic-human-existence","itemId":"42bd1029-7f56-5b8d-824a-92f2405670f1","before":{"title":"Philosophy, Logic and Human Existence","body":"A foundational course examining what philosophy is and why it matters, moving from its nature and scope through the classical branches — metaphysics, epistemology, ethics and aesthetics, logic and argument, and the philosophy of other disciplines — before turning outward to socio-political philosophy, African philoso$owner_content_rule$);
insert into private.content_rule_chunks (rule, ordinal, chunk) values ('owner_content_rule', 32, $owner_content_rule$phy, the philosophy and history of science, and philosophy's broader relevance to technology and society.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"Conception, Nature, Scope and Problems of Philosophy"},{"chapter":"2","description":"A Survey of the History of Philosophy"},{"chapter":"3","description":"Metaphysics as a Branch of Philosophy"},{"chapter":"4","description":"Epistemology as a Branch of Philosophy"},{"chapter":"5","description":"Ethics and Aesthetics"},{"chapter":"6","description":"Logic and Its Importance"},{"chapter":"7","description":"Inference and Argument Types in Logic"},{"chapter":"8","description":"Laws of Thought, Elementary Formal Rules of Reasoning and Fallacies"},{"chapter":"9","description":"The Philosophy of Other Disciplines"},{"chapter":"10","description":"Socio-Political Philosophy"},{"chapter":"11","description":"African Philosophy"},{"chapter":"12","description":"The Concept and Nature of Philosophy of Science"},{"chapter":"13","description":"The History and Method of Science"},{"chapter":"14","description":"Philosophy, Science and Technology"},{"chapter":"15","description":"Philosophy and Its Relevance to Society"}],"examFocus":["The distinction between the narrow (technical) and broad (worldview) senses of philosophy","Why 'What is philosophy?' is treated as philosophy's own most fundamental problem","The shift from Pre-Socratic cosmology (Thales, Anaximander, Heraclitus, Parmenides) to Socrates' turn toward ethics","Why metaphysics lost independent status to the positive sciences, and how it remains their 'ground'","The rationalist vs empiricist divide, and Plato's account of knowledge as acquaintance with Forms","The three subdivisions of ethics (descriptive, meta-ethics, normative) and teleological vs deontological theories","Why logic is not the same discipline as the psychology of reasoning","The distinction between deductive validity and inductive strength/correctness","The three Laws of Thought (Identity, Contradiction, Excluded Middle) and their symbolic forms","How philosophy functions as the 'infrastructure' underlying law, history, religion, and science","Why prescribing the organisation of the ideal state is socio-political philosophy's central task","The narrow and broad senses of philosophy in African philosophy, and its four core content areas","Why philosophy should not become subservient ('handmaiden') to science, and what 'Philosophical Science' means","The trajectory of Greek scientific thought from Thales through Plato and Aristotle","Why philosophy's relevance to society lies in providing an organised, coherent view of a disjointed world"]},"after":{"title":"Philosophy, Logic and Human Existence","body":"A foundational course examining what philosophy is and why it matters, moving from its nature and scope through the classical branches — metaphysics, epistemology, ethics and aesthetics, logic and argument, and the philosophy of other disciplines — before turning outward to socio-political philosophy, African philosophy, the philosophy and history of science, and philosophy's broader relevance to technology and society.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"Conception, Nature, Scope and Problems of Philosophy"},{"chapter":"2","description":"A Survey of the History of Philosophy"},{"chapter":"3","description":"Metaphysics as a Branch of Philosophy"},{"chapter":"4","description":"Epistemology as a Branch of Philosophy"},{"chapter":"5","description":"Ethics and Aesthetics"},{"chapter":"6","description":"Logic and Its Importance"},{"chapter":"7","description":"Inference and Argument Types in Logic"},{"chapter":"8","description":"Laws of Thought, Elementary Formal Rules of Reasoning and Fallacies"},{"chapter":"9","description":"The Philosophy of Other Disciplines"},{"chapter":"10","description":"Socio-Political Philosophy"},{"chapter":"11","description":"African Philosophy"},{"chapter":"12","description":"The Concept and Nature of Philosophy of Science"},{"chapter":"13","description":"The History and Method of Science"},{"chapter":"14","description":"Philosophy, Science and Technology"},{"chapter":"15","description":"Philosophy and Its Relevance to Society"}]}},{"slug":"introduction-financial-management","itemId":"aacda638-71d7-590e-9728-205fc7214117","before":{"title":"Introduction to Financial Management","body":"A quantitative course in financial decision-making. The course progresses from the time value of money through investment appraisal (with certainty, risk, rationing, inflation, and tax), the cost of capital and leverage, working capital management, portfolio theory, dividend policy, and financial statement analysis, before moving into capital market instruments — bond valuation and the Capital Asset Pricing Model.","examFocus":["The difference between ordinary annuity and annuity due, and correctly identifying which applies to a given cash flow pattern","Continuous compounding versus discrete compounding, and computing the effective annual rate from a nominal rate","Resolving NPV/IRR conflicts for mutually exclusive projects with differing cash flow timing — and why NPV is the theoretically preferred criterion","Sensitivity margin calculations for sales, cost, and asset price in a capital budgeting decision, and the method's key limitation","Using the risk-free (treasury bill) rate, not the risk-adjusted cost of capital, to discount expected cash flows when computing Expected NPV","Coefficient of variation versus standard deviation as the correct basis for choosing between investments of different scale for a risk-averse investor","The Economic Order Quantity (EOQ) formula, and correctly separating ordering/carrying cost (controllable) from purchase cost (largely fixed)","The cash discount cost formula — [%disc ÷ (100 − %disc)] × [365 ÷ (max payment period − discount period)] — and its sensitivity to the assumed days-in-year convention (360 vs 365)","Computing a firm's working capital cycle in days from stock, debtor, WIP, and creditor turnover periods","Portfolio return as a weighted average of individual security returns, versus portfolio risk, which is NOT a simple weighted average once correlation is introduced","Identifying the minimum-risk portfolio weighting, which is rarely a 100% holding in either security","How positive correlation between two securities reduces the diversification benefit of combining them","The four major categories of financial ratio (liquidity, profitability/efficiency, long-term solvency and stability, stock market/investor) and which ratios belong to each","Correctly identifying all components of current liabilities in a ratio question — tax payable is often a hidden addition to trade creditors, not stated as a separate line","Bond valuation with semi-annual coupons — the periodic discount rate must be halved (Kd ÷ 2) and the number of periods doubled (2n)","The CAPM formula E(Ri) = Rf + [E(Rm) − Rf]×Bi, and the decision rule for over-priced versus $owner_content_rule$);
